Fixed. Here's what was actually wrong and what I did.

## Root cause

Not a PATH problem or a broken `pip` script — your `/usr/local/bin/pip` was fine. The **importable `pip` package directory had been deleted** from site-packages while its metadata was left behind:

```
/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/
├── README.txt
└── pip-24.3.1.dist-info   ← metadata present, claimed pip was installed
```

`pip` therefore crashed at startup on `from pip._internal.cli.main import main` → `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`.

Two things compounded it:
- The **orphaned `dist-info` made `ensurepip` refuse to repair**, reporting `Requirement already satisfied` while the code was still absent.
- Nothing else blocked you: no `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` marker for 3.13, site-packages writable, 221 GB free.

Directory timestamps suggest the `pip/` folder was removed around **Oct 31 13:45** (parent dir modified then) while the Jan 2025 metadata survived — typical of an interrupted upgrade or a partial delete.

## Fix

1. Backed up the orphaned metadata to `/tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.bak`.
2. Moved it aside to `/tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.orphan`.
3. Re-ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip`, which installed pip from the bundled `pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl` — **no network required**.

## Verified

| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| `pip --version` / `pip3 --version` | `pip 24.3.1 ... (python 3.13)` |
| `python3 -m pip --version` | works |
| `import pip` | resolves to `.../site-packages/pip/__init__.py` |
| `pip check` | No broken requirements found |
| PyPI index access | reachable (`six` 1.17.0 listed) |
| Real install | `Successfully installed six-1.17.0` |
| Uninstall | `Successfully uninstalled six-1.17.0` |

I uninstalled the test package afterward, so your environment is left as I found it apart from the repair.

## Two things worth knowing

- **`/usr/bin/python3` (Debian's 3.11) has no pip at all** — a separate install from the one you were hitting. If you need it, that's `apt install python3-pip`, not `ensurepip`; note it carries an `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` marker, so it resists system-wide installs by design. The `python3`/`pip` on your `PATH` is the 3.13 one I just repaired.
- You're running as **root**, which is why every command printed pip's "Running pip as the 'root' user" warning. For actual project work prefer a venv (`python3 -m venv .venv`) to avoid this class of damage recurring — but system-wide installs will work now.

Once you're happy, you can delete the two backups: `rm -rf /tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.bak /tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.orphan`. I left them in place in case you want to inspect them.
